WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:11.540
I'm Christine Boinett.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:13.800
I am a post-doc working in pathogen genomics at the

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:18.120
Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute in
Cambridge. My work involves looking at bacteria

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.740
in particular ones that are a problem in
hospitals because they're resistant to

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:27.120
so many antibiotics that doctors commonly use.
I'm using a combination of lab work

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.780
DNA sequencing and computational work to
understand how bacteria become resistant

00:00:31.789 --> 00:00:33.809
to antibiotics.

00:00:33.809 --> 00:00:37.460
I want to understand if resistance is
something they acquire

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:42.880
or if it's just naturally there within
them. While half my time is more informatics based

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.340
the other half is spent in the lab.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:49.020
In the lab I grow up bacteria on agar plates and then

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.120
see how they respond to different antibiotics

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.220
While some bugs will be killed by the antibiotics

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:58.800
others will be resistant and these are the
ones that i'm interested in.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:03.550
I like the hands-on element of working in the
lab -

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:04.910
setting up the experiments and then

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:08.060
coming back the next day and discovering the results.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:13.660
When I find the bugs that are resistant
to antibiotics, I isolate their DNA

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.480
I then get this DNA sequenced

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.340
 

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:24.040
Back on my computer, I then compare
this DNA sequence to a database of genes

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.470
known to be involved in antibiotic
resistance.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.160
I can then see if any of these
resistance genes are in my sample.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.780
More and more I'm enjoying the computational side of my research

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:39.040
because it enables me to
really pinpoint the mechanisms of

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:40.980
antibiotic resistance.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.820
I always enjoyed science in school but
really got fascinated about

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.100
drug resistance when I was doing my degree.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:52.200
During my degree I spent a year in
industry looking at drug resistance in HIV.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:56.480
Understanding how one mutation can cause
resistance to a whole class of anti-virals

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:59.800
was really intriguing to me.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:03.100
I've studied the mechanisms of drug resistance
in bacteria ever since

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:08.150
first for my PhD and now here at the Sanger Institute.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.840
I really enjoy working in research

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:14.030
I get to collaborate with lots of very
accomplished scientists

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:18.690
and they challenge me every day, to me that's how you really grow as a scientist.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:22.800
Drug resistance is fascinating to me, I just want to find out

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.540
more about how it develops and how we might overcome it.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.380
I love what I do!

